<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kagoshima Prefecture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a> Prefecture is a prefecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> at the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> and Osumi peninsulas and a chain of islands reaching towards Okinawa. It was created in 1871 from the lands of the Satsuma Domain, whose Shimazu lords had ruled the region for centuries. Its capital is the city of Kagoshima, facing the active volcano Sakurajima.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kagoshima-prefecture.jpg" alt="Kagoshima Prefecture" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Modern Polymath · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2025-09-08_Sakurajima_Sunrise.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kagoshima Prefecture lies at the south-western tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> and Ōsumi peninsulas, which are separated by Kagoshima Bay. It also includes a chain of islands, most importantly the Amami Islands, stretching a few hundred kilometres to the south-west, so that its coastline runs to 2,632 kilometres including 28 islands. Its position made it a gateway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> at various times in history. Active and dormant volcanoes are found across the prefecture. Sakurajima, which rises out of Kagoshima Bay opposite the city, sends up a steady trickle of smoke and ash. In its major eruption of 1914 the island mountain spilled enough material to become permanently joined to the mainland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The prefecture corresponds to the ancient provinces of Ōsumi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>, together with the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyu-islands">Ryukyu Islands</a>, known as Satsunan. Its territory formed the core of the Satsuma Domain, ruled from Kagoshima Castle, one of the most important domains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>. The region did much to bring about that Restoration through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a>, and the city of Kagoshima was an important naval base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s wars of the 20th century and the home of Admiral Tōgō Heihachirō. The Satsuma lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-nariakira">Shimazu Nariakira</a> is said to have introduced the deep-fried fish cake satsuma-age from the Ryūkyū Kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima">Kagoshima</a> is the capital and largest city, and most of the economy is concentrated there and in the area of the former Satsuma Province. The eastern part, the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osumi-province">Ōsumi Province</a>, is mostly rural. The prefecture has strong agricultural roots, and its best-known products include green <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweet-potato">sweet potatoes</a>, radishes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berkshire">Berkshire</a> pork and Black Wagyu beef. It has more than a hundred distilleries of shōchū, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s most traditional drinks. The Japan Aerospace Exploration Agency has facilities here, including the country's main launch site on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanegashima">Tanegashima</a>. The people speak the distinctive Satsugū dialect, mutually unintelligible with standard Japanese, though most speakers know both.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kagoshima Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It became <b>Kagoshima Prefecture</b> in 1871; Saigo's Satsuma Rebellion of 1877 was crushed.</blockquote><a href="/state/satsuma-domain">Satsuma Domain</a></li></ul></section>
